<h2 id='key-features'>⚙️ Key Features for Tour Operators</h2>

<h3>Multi-Currency Invoicing</h3>
<p>ProfitBooks supports 150+ currencies with live exchange rate updates. You can invoice a client in GBP, record the revenue in USD as your base currency, and have the FX difference automatically logged. For operators selling to international markets or paying overseas ground handlers, this eliminates the manual conversion work that creates reconciliation errors. You can also lock exchange rates on specific invoices — useful when you've guaranteed a client price but settlement is weeks away.</p>

<h3>Client & Vendor Management</h3>
<p>The contact management module handles both customers and suppliers in a unified ledger. Each contact has a transaction history, outstanding balance view, and payment status. For tour operators managing relationships with accommodation partners, transport providers, and local guides, this gives you a clean payables dashboard without needing a separate CRM for financial tracking. It's not a substitute for a full <a href='https://traveloperator.io/reviews/best-crm-for-tour-operators'>CRM built for tour operators</a>, but it handles the financial side competently.</p>

<h3>Invoicing & Payment Tracking</h3>
<p>Invoice creation is fast. You can build templates, add line items for tour components, apply taxes, and send directly from the platform. Payment reminders are automated, and the system tracks partial payments — relevant for operators who collect deposits and balance payments on different schedules. Online payment links (via Stripe and PayPal) can be embedded in invoices, reducing the back-and-forth that delays cash collection.</p>

<h3>Expense Management</h3>
<p>Expense entry is straightforward: categorize, attach a receipt, assign to a vendor, and mark as billable if it's a reimbursable client cost. You can set recurring expenses for fixed supplier contracts — useful for operators with standing agreements for accommodation blocks or vehicle hire. The mobile app supports receipt scanning, which matters when your guides are submitting expense claims from the field.</p>

<h3>Financial Reporting</h3>
<p>ProfitBooks generates standard reports: P&L, balance sheet, cash flow statement, trial balance, and aging receivables/payables. Reports can be filtered by date range and exported to PDF or Excel. What's missing is report customization — you can't easily build a per-tour profitability view without manual exports and manipulation. For operators who want per-departure margin reporting, this is a limitation that Xero handles better.</p>

<h3>Inventory Management</h3>
<p>Included at no extra cost, inventory tracking is more relevant for operators who sell merchandise, equipment rentals, or consumable tour supplies. It's not a complex warehouse system, but it handles stock-level tracking, purchase orders, and basic inventory valuation — enough for small operators who want to monitor gear depreciation or kit resupply cycles.</p>

<div class='callout-orange'>
  <strong>⚠️ Warning:</strong> ProfitBooks' payroll module is primarily configured for Indian labor law compliance. If you're running payroll for guides, drivers, or office staff in the US, UK, Australia, or Europe, you'll need a separate payroll tool. Don't assume payroll coverage extends globally — it doesn't in any practical sense.
</div>

